  "decision_conditions": [
    "The development hereby permitted must be begun not later than the end of three years from the date of this permission. \r\rReason: To comply with the provisions of S.51 of the Planning & Compulsory Purchase Act 2004.\r",
    "The development hereby permitted shall be carried out in accordance with the following approved plans:\r\rArchaeological Desk Based Assessment - North Middlesex Hospital, dated September 2025 \rDatums -1702-D \rDatums - to be demolished 1702-D\rElevations 1702-E\rElevations - to be demolished 1702-E\rVIS-NMH-001-HTA-DR-A-0100\rVIS-NMH-001-HTA-A-DR-0001\rVIS-NMH-001-HTA-A-DR-0202\rVIS-NMH-001-HTA-A-DR-0200\rFlood Risk Assessment, dated September 2025 Rev P02\rDemolition Logistics Plan and Transport Statement dated 2 October 2025 \rTechnical Notes, dated 01/10/2025 Rev: 01 \rDesk Study and Ground Investigation Report dated 29 December 2021 \rPreliminary Ecological Appraisal dated September 2025 \r3139-MWL-ZZ-00-DR-E-6211 Rev P01 \rTree Condition Survey for Land at North Middlesex University Hospital, dated September 2025 \r\rReason: For the avoidance of doubt and in the interests of proper planning.\r",
    "All Non-Road Mobile Machinery (NRMM) of net power of 37kW and up to and including 560kW used during the course of the demolition, site preparation and construction phases shall comply with the emission standards set out in chapter 7 of the GLA's supplementary planning guidance \"Control of Dust and Emissions During Construction and Demolition\" dated July 2014 (SPG), or subsequent guidance. Unless it complies with the standards set out in the SPG, no NRMM shall be on site, at any time, whether in use or not, without the prior written consent of the local planning authority. The developer shall keep an up to date list of all NRMM used during the demolition, site preparation and construction phases of the development on the online register at https://nrmm.london/.\r\rReasons: In the interests of good air quality.\r",
    "Prior to the demolition of Blocks C and D on the site, the developer shall submit a Demolition Logistics Management Plan (DLMP) to the Planning Authority for approval. The DLMP shall address the following key aspects:\r\rSite Access and Egress\ro\tDetail the proposed routes for demolition vehicles to access and exit the site.\ro\tProvide measures to minimise traffic congestion and disruptions to the surrounding road network.\ro\tSpecify any proposed temporary road closures or diversions, if applicable.\r\rDemolition Traffic Management:\ro\tOutline arrangements for the management of demolition traffic, including loading and unloading areas.\ro\tConfirmation of designated times for deliveries to minimise impact on peak traffic hours.\ro\tProvide measures to ensure the safe passage of pedestrians and cyclists around the site.\r\rSite Layout and Storage:\ro\tProvide a site layout plan indicating the location of demolition activities, storage areas, and welfare facilities.\ro\tDetail provisions for the storage of materials, equipment, and waste to minimise visual and environmental impact.\ro\tSpecify measures to mitigate noise, dust, and other nuisances generated by demolition activities.\r\rDemolition Schedule:\ro\tPresent a demolition schedule outlining the sequence of works and anticipated duration of each phase, including number and type of vehicles required for each stage.\ro\tDetail the hours of work expected for each phase and the overall duration.\r\rHealth and Safety:\ro\tDemonstrate compliance with health and safety regulations, including provisions for worker safety and welfare.\ro\tDetail measures to mitigate risks associated with demolition activities, such as traffic accidents and site hazards.\ro\tArrangements for wheel cleaning.\ro\tMeasures to use operators committed to best practice, demonstrated by membership of TfL's Freight Operator Recognition Scheme (FORS), or similar\r\rReason: To ensure no adverse impact on the local highway and to protect the local amenity from demolition/construction dust. \r",
    "Prior to the demolition of Blocks C and D, a scheme compliant with BS 5837:2012  for the retention and protection of trees identified as T1, T2, T3, T4, T5, T6, T7 and T47 in the Ruskins Tree Survey Ref 0925-1008970 dated September 2025, shall be submitted to and approved in writing by the Local Planning Authority. The scheme will comprise a detailed Arboricultural Method Statement (AMS) report supplemented by Tree Protection Plan(s) (TPP). \r\rSpecific matters to be addressed within the AMS and TPPs will include but not be limited to:\ra)\tmethods of demolition of existing structures within or directly adjacent to RPAs and the removal of existing hard standing and kerb edging within or directly adjacent to RPAs;\rb)\ta specification for protective fencing and, if necessary, ground protection, to safeguard all trees during demolition. The Tree Protection Plan(s) shall clearly indicate the alignment of the fencing and extent of ground protection;\rc)\ta specification for the design and installation of site hoarding where it is located within the RPAs of retained trees.\rd)\tdetails of site access, temporary parking, on site welfare facilities, loading, unloading and storage of equipment, materials, fuels and waste as well concrete mixing and siting of any fires;\r\rThe demolition thereafter shall be implemented in accordance with the approved details.\r\rReason: To protect existing trees. \r"
  ],
